<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <BrandHead
        title="What true product code looks like — Fluxzero"
        description="See how Fluxzero turns product intent into real, production-ready code without service, persistence, transaction, messaging, or workflow scaffolding."
    />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/v4.css?v=20260905-1">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/technical-foundation.css?v=20260904-5">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/technical-foundation-next.css?v=20260910-11">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/product-code.css?v=20260911-3">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/marketing-shell.css?v=20260903-1">
</head>
<body class="v4-page cloud-proof-page cloud-proof-next-page product-code-page">
    <MarketingHeader active="how-it-works" />

    <main>
        <header class="cloud-proof-hero cloud-proof-next-hero product-code-hero">
            <div class="v4-shell cloud-proof-shell product-code-hero__layout">
                <div class="product-code-hero__copy">
                    <p class="cloud-proof-hero__back" data-reveal><a href="/how-it-works#behavior-title" data-browser-back><i class="bx bx-left-arrow-alt" aria-hidden="true"></i> Back</a></p>
                    <p class="cloud-proof-next__eyebrow" data-reveal>Product code</p>
                    <h1 data-reveal data-reveal-delay="1">The code starts with<br><span>what the customer wants.</span></h1>
                    <p class="cloud-proof-hero__lead" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="2">Most application code describes machinery. In Fluxzero, customer actions, product rules, state changes, tests, and reactions are the architecture. Your agent can spend its effort on the behavior people actually use.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="product-code-hero__visual" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="2" data-llms-exclude>
                    <ProductCodePanel class="product-code-panel--hero" title="ReserveTicket.java" label="The starting point" code={heroCode} />
                </div>
            </div>
        </header>

        <article class="cloud-proof-section cloud-proof-next-section product-code-section" id="intent" aria-labelledby="intent-title">
            <div class="v4-shell cloud-proof-shell">
                <header class="cloud-proof-copy" data-reveal>
                    <p class="cloud-proof-next__number">01 · Intent first</p>
                    <h2 id="intent-title">The first file names<br><span>a customer decision.</span></h2>
                    <p>Start with <code>ReserveTicket</code>, not with a schema or service layer. Its payload contains only the intent needed to perform the action. The customer is already part of the authenticated message context and is injected as <code>User</code>.</p>
                    <p>The rule, the ticket change, and the new reservation are evaluated as one decision. If any part fails, none of it is committed.</p>
                </header>
                <div class="product-code-proof product-code-proof--action" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="1">
                    <ProductCodePanel title="ReserveTicket.java" label="Action, rule, two changes" code={actionCode} />
                    <dl class="product-code-proof__notes">
                        <div><dt>Pure intent</dt><dd>No caller-supplied customer identity and no infrastructure parameters.</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>Current context</dt><dd>The authenticated customer is injected by Fluxzero.</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>One decision</dt><dd>The ticket and reservation change together—or not at all.</dd></div>
                    </dl>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="cloud-proof-section cloud-proof-next-section product-code-section" id="domain-state" aria-labelledby="domain-state-title">
            <div class="v4-shell cloud-proof-shell">
                <header class="cloud-proof-copy" data-reveal>
                    <p class="cloud-proof-next__number">02 · State follows behavior</p>
                    <h2 id="domain-state-title">State holders follow the decisions.<br><span>Not the other way around.</span></h2>
                    <p>A show has sellable tickets. A ticket contains one seat and may have a reservation. The seat is a value inside the ticket; the reservation has its own lifecycle and history. That gives the model one clear chain instead of three competing sources of truth.</p>
                    <p>Each independent model is stored and loaded by its typed identity. Relationships remain explicit without forcing an entire object tree into memory.</p>
                </header>
                <div class="product-code-proof product-code-proof--model" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="1">
                    <ProductCodePanel title="TicketingModel.java" label="State holders and relationships" code={modelCode} />
                    <dl class="product-code-proof__notes">
                        <div><dt>Show → Ticket</dt><dd>A ticket is inventory for one show.</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>Ticket → Reservation</dt><dd>A reservation lives independently but remains linked to its ticket.</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>Seat</dt><dd>A value object inside the ticket, because it has no separate lifecycle here.</dd></div>
                    </dl>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="cloud-proof-section cloud-proof-next-section product-code-section" id="tests" aria-labelledby="tests-title">
            <div class="v4-shell cloud-proof-shell">
                <header class="cloud-proof-copy" data-reveal>
                    <p class="cloud-proof-next__number">03 · Behavior as proof</p>
                    <h2 id="tests-title">Prove the product behavior<br><span>before an endpoint exists.</span></h2>
                    <p>The fixture runs the same command and model pipeline as the application. Tests state what already happened, who acts next, and what must follow. There are no repositories, queues, clocks, or transaction managers to mock.</p>
                </header>
                <div class="product-code-proof product-code-proof--test" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="1">
                    <ProductCodePanel title="ReserveTicketTest.java" label="Real user, real model pipeline" code={testCode} />
                    <dl class="product-code-proof__notes">
                        <div><dt>Accepted</dt><dd>The ticket is reserved and the reservation belongs to customer 42.</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>Rejected</dt><dd>A second customer cannot claim the same ticket.</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>Observable</dt><dd>The accepted command is the event; no duplicate event class is invented.</dd></div>
                    </dl>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="cloud-proof-section cloud-proof-next-section product-code-section" id="search" aria-labelledby="search-title">
            <div class="v4-shell cloud-proof-shell">
                <header class="cloud-proof-copy" data-reveal>
                    <p class="cloud-proof-next__number">04 · Product questions</p>
                    <h2 id="search-title">Ask the product.<br><span>Not the database.</span></h2>
                    <p>The query asks for available balcony tickets for one show, ordered by seat row. The terms come directly from the model. Fluxzero owns the indexed document, relationship lookup, and asynchronous retrieval behind them. <code>@Sortable</code> is an optional index that keeps repeated row sorting fast.</p>
                </header>
                <div class="product-code-proof product-code-proof--query" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="1">
                    <ProductCodePanel title="FindAvailableTickets.java" label="Typed asynchronous query" code={searchCode} />
                    <div class="product-code-query" aria-label="Available tickets are selected within a show and section, then ordered by row">
                        <span><small>Within</small><strong>Show</strong></span>
                        <i class="bx bx-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        <span><small>Status</small><strong>Available</strong></span>
                        <i class="bx bx-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        <span><small>Section</small><strong>Balcony</strong></span>
                        <i class="bx bx-right-arrow-alt" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        <span class="is-result"><small>Order by</small><strong>Seat row</strong></span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="cloud-proof-section cloud-proof-next-section product-code-section" id="workflows" aria-labelledby="workflows-title">
            <div class="v4-shell cloud-proof-shell">
                <header class="cloud-proof-copy" data-reveal>
                    <p class="cloud-proof-next__number">05 · Time without plumbing</p>
                    <h2 id="workflows-title">Time can pass without changing<br><span>the programming model.</span></h2>
                    <p>Reserving starts a durable fifteen-minute timer. Payment cancels it. If it fires, an ordinary command expires the reservation and releases the ticket in one atomic decision. The code describes the deadline and its product outcome, not scheduler infrastructure.</p>
                </header>
                <div class="product-code-interactions product-code-interactions--workflow" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="1">
                    <ProductCodePanel title="ReservationTimers.java" label="Start and cancel a deadline" code={timerCode} />
                    <ProductCodePanel title="ExpireReservation.java" label="Close and release together" code={expiryCode} />
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="cloud-proof-section cloud-proof-next-section product-code-section" id="policy" aria-labelledby="policy-title">
            <div class="v4-shell cloud-proof-shell">
                <header class="cloud-proof-copy" data-reveal>
                    <p class="cloud-proof-next__number">06 · Policy in context</p>
                    <h2 id="policy-title">Identity and sensitive data stay beside<br><span>the behavior they govern.</span></h2>
                    <p>The ticketing domain requires an authenticated user by default. A reservation rule compares that injected identity with its owner, so the command cannot impersonate another customer. Personal contact data is validated and kept outside the durable message payload without a second storage design.</p>
                </header>
                <div class="product-code-interactions product-code-interactions--policy" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="1">
                    <ProductCodePanel title="package-info.java" label="Secure by default" code={packagePolicyCode} />
                    <ProductCodePanel title="UpdateContactEmail.java" label="Ownership and protected data" code={personalDataCode} />
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="cloud-proof-section cloud-proof-next-section product-code-section" id="web" aria-labelledby="web-title">
            <div class="v4-shell cloud-proof-shell">
                <header class="cloud-proof-copy" data-reveal>
                    <p class="cloud-proof-next__number">07 · The endpoint comes last</p>
                    <h2 id="web-title">The API is a thin entrance.<br><span>Not another architecture.</span></h2>
                    <p>The endpoint turns web input into the same <code>ReserveTicket</code> action the tests already proved. It creates the new identity at the boundary and returns it. The current user flows with the command automatically; there is no controller-service-repository chain to recreate.</p>
                </header>
                <div class="product-code-proof product-code-proof--web" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="1">
                    <ProductCodePanel title="ReservationApi.java" label="HTTP to product action" code={webCode} />
                    <dl class="product-code-proof__notes">
                        <div><dt>Input</dt><dd>The request only identifies the ticket the customer chose.</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>Action</dt><dd>The endpoint sends the already-tested product command.</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>Output</dt><dd>Ordinary return values and failures become web responses.</dd></div>
                    </dl>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="cloud-proof-section cloud-proof-next-section product-code-section" id="evolution" aria-labelledby="evolution-title">
            <div class="v4-shell cloud-proof-shell">
                <header class="cloud-proof-copy" data-reveal>
                    <p class="cloud-proof-next__number">08 · The past stays useful</p>
                    <h2 id="evolution-title">New capabilities can begin with<br><span>the product’s first event.</span></h2>
                    <p>Add rewards after a million confirmed reservations and let the new capability consume the history from index zero. Each event arrives with the reservation state exactly as it was after that decision, so new code can use old product behavior without rebuilding a data migration pipeline.</p>
                </header>
                <div class="product-code-proof product-code-proof--history" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="1">
                    <ProductCodePanel title="CustomerRewards.java" label="New capability, complete history" code={historyCode} />
                    <dl class="product-code-proof__notes">
                        <div><dt>Then</dt><dd>Every confirmed reservation is already in the event history.</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>Now</dt><dd>The rewards capability is added without changing ticketing.</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>From the start</dt><dd><code>minIndex = 0</code> lets it process the complete story.</dd></div>
                    </dl>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <section class="cloud-proof-next-conclusion product-code-conclusion" aria-labelledby="conclusion-title">
            <div class="v4-shell cloud-proof-shell" data-reveal>
                <p class="cloud-proof-next__eyebrow">The result</p>
                <h2 id="conclusion-title">Write the product.<br><span>Inherit the machinery.</span></h2>
                <p>Your agent starts with decisions customers understand and keeps working in that language as the product gains state, policy, search, time, APIs, and new capabilities.</p>
                <strong>Less scaffolding in the prompt. More product in the code.</strong>
                <div class="cloud-proof-next__actions">
                    <a class="v4-button v4-button--primary" href="/get-started"><span>Start building</span><i class="bx bx-right-arrow-alt" aria-hidden="true"></i></a>
                    <a class="v4-button v4-button--secondary" href="/docs"><span>Inspect the SDK</span><i class="bx bx-right-arrow-alt" aria-hidden="true"></i></a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>

    <MarketingFooter />
    <script src="/js/v4.js?v=20260904-3" defer></script>
</body>
</html>
